<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for Gezel.app and, via `entitlementsInherit`,
  every Electron helper inside it.

  Each entry here gives back a protection hardened runtime otherwise grants for
  free, so the set is deliberately minimal: an entitlement stays only while a
  concrete thing in the payload needs it. scripts/macos-entitlements.test.mjs
  pins the list — adding one is a deliberate, reviewed act, not a drive-by.

  Four unnecessary entitlements were removed in the v1.26211.26 release
  audit:

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      An Electron 11-and-older compatibility exception. Electron 12+ uses the
      narrower MAP_JIT path authorized by allow-jit on supported Darwin
      architectures; @electron/notarize explicitly says not to grant unsigned
      executable memory on newer releases because it increases attack surface.
      scripts/macos-entitlements.test.mjs pins that Electron-version floor.

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables
      Previously justified as needed to spawn gezeld from the extracted
      service bundle. That reasoning was wrong: this entitlement controls
      whether *this* process honours DYLD_* variables handed to it, and has no
      bearing on execing a child. Nothing in the tree sets a DYLD_ variable,
      and native engines locate their sibling dylibs through @loader_path /
      @rpath load commands rather than the environment. All it bought was a
      DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES injection path into a signed process.

    com.apple.security.network.client / .network.server
      App Sandbox capability keys. Gezel is deliberately not App Sandbox-
      enabled: its local-first model requires user-selected workspaces, local
      tools and child processes. Without com.apple.security.app-sandbox these
      keys do not gate ordinary loopback or outbound sockets, so they granted
      nothing and incorrectly made the hardened-runtime set look larger.

  If a future change needs any of them back, the reason belongs here in writing —
  and scripts/verify-macos-entitlements.mjs must continue to confirm the
  effective signed payload, not just this source file.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- V8 and the Chromium renderers JIT-compile. Supported Electron 12+
         releases use the narrower MAP_JIT path authorized by this entitlement;
         Electron cannot start without it. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Library validation would require every Mach-O loaded into the process
         to carry Bendyline's team ID. Two things in the payload legitimately
         do not:

           1. scripts/sign-macho-tree.mjs preserves a third-party binary that
              already ships a valid distribution Developer ID signature
              byte-for-byte, rather than re-signing vendor code as ours. Those
              carry their vendor's team ID by design.
           2. Toolsets and Python packages installed at runtime (pnpm into
              ~/.gezel/system-toolsets, uv into ~/.gezel/python) bring prebuilt
              native addons that no one signed for us and that cannot be signed
              at build time, because they are fetched after the build.

         This is the one entitlement here that genuinely widens what may enter
         the process, and it is load-bearing: without it those loads fail at
         dlopen. Narrowing it means changing what gets loaded, not the plist. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
